# Odile — the bench liveness probe: one line, every five minutes. ODL-936.
#
# On 2026-11-21 the bench answered 404 for half a day while /health answered
# 200: odile-bench.service had restarted 17,060 times because an orphaned
# seed.py serve from a deleted card worktree held 127.0.0.1:8082. This oneshot
# runs `scripts/check_bench.sh --probe`; a red line is raised in the existing
# HQ alert ledger and outbox (odilectl.health), so a dead bench is an alert
# rather than a page nobody reads.
#
# ExecStart is `--probe-tick`, not the probe itself: the tick prints the one
# line and feeds the alert path, while the probe stays a plain manual script.
#
# ODL-353 follow-up — the boot race. `odile-bench-probe.timer` is Persistent,
# so the tick missed during a reboot runs the moment the timer starts; this
# unit is ordered `After=odile-bench.service`, but that unit is Type=simple and
# systemd marks it active when app.py is exec'd, before it binds
# 127.0.0.1:8082. On 2026-11-25T04:55:41Z the catch-up tick and the first bind
# met in the same second, the tick measured a Caddy 502, and this unit was the
# boot record's only failed one. The tick now waits for the bench's listener
# (`--probe-tick` → `wait_for_bench`, bounded by BENCH_WAIT_SECONDS in
# deploy_bench.py) before it probes. The wait is not a failure path: a bench
# that never binds still probes red and still raises the alert, and the timeout
# below leaves room for the 20 s wait plus the probe's own 90 s.
[Unit]
Description=Odile bench liveness probe — test.odile.fyi (ODL-936)
Documentation=https://odile.fyi
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target odile-bench.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=root
WorkingDirectory={{ROOT}}/current
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 {{ROOT}}/current/deploy/bench/deploy_bench.py --probe-tick
Nice=10
NoNewPrivileges=yes
PrivateTmp=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=read-only
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/odile
TimeoutStartSec=180
